# GPT-5.3-Codex-Spark — findings by Qwen 3.8 27B (SELF-EXCLUDED)

- Source: OpenAI/gpt-5.3-codex-spark, e.g. OpenCode Zen `opencode/gpt-5.3-codex-spark`
- Date: 2026-10-01 (UTC)
- Overview and scoring methodology: `../../model-comparison.md`
- Cross-model signed log: `../../model-findings.md`

## Model card

- **Name:** GPT-5.3-Codex-Spark
- **Short description:** OpenAI's "ultra-fast model for real-time coding in Codex" — a smaller version of GPT-5.3-Codex and OpenAI's first model designed for real-time coding; research preview (Feb–Sep 2026), deprecated from Codex clients on 2026-09-14 but still served on the OpenAI API and OpenCode Zen.
- **Provider / access:** research preview for ChatGPT Pro users in the Codex app, CLI, and VS Code/IDE extension (Feb 2026); API initially for a small set of design partners, later generally available via the OpenAI API and OpenCode Zen (`gpt-5.3-codex-spark`), plus a Poe listing. Responses-API style tool calling; reasoning, structured outputs supported.
- **Release / knowledge:** research preview launched February 2026 (OpenAI post dated 2026-02-12; models.dev release date 2026-02-05); knowledge 2025-08 (models.dev). Deprecated in Codex clients 2026-09-14 (ChatGPT Learn changelog "GPT-5.3-Codex-Spark deprecated").
- **IDs:** `gpt-5.3-codex-spark` (OpenAI API / OpenCode Zen; family `gpt-codex-spark` on models.dev). No Free ID verified in this pass.
- **Context window:** 128,000 total (128k context, 32,000 max output — OpenAI launch post + models.dev).
- **Modalities:** text in / text out at launch ("text-only at a 128k context window" — OpenAI launch post; models.dev lists no image input).
- **Pricing (as of 2026-10-01):** $1.75 / $14.00 per 1M input/output tokens on the OpenAI API (models.dev; matches GPT-5.3-Codex sticker).
- **Architecture / serving:** proprietary; served on Cerebras Wafer Scale Engine 3 — the first milestone of the OpenAI–Cerebras partnership (announced Jan 2026); >1000 tokens/sec sampling speed, latency-first tier.
- **Default behavior:** lightweight working style — minimal, targeted edits; does not automatically run tests unless asked (OpenAI launch post).

